0.00225830078125, 0.01450347900390625, 0.01248931884765625, 0.0205078125, 0.0064697265625, 0.0255126953125, -0.0289764404296875, -0.0181884765625, 0.007251739501953125, -0.006671905517578125, -0.004360198974609375, 0.004436492919921875, 0.010528564453125, -0.0032958984375, 0.0018739700317382812, -0.017913818359375, 0.01047515869140625, 0.035125732421875, 0.02294921875, 0.0048980712890625, -0.02142333984375, 0.00945281982421875, -0.0193023681640625, 0.01287078857421875, -0.0167999267578125, 0.004974365234375, -0.007717132568359375, -0.028778076171875, 0.0045928955078125, -0.007305145263671875, 0.014801025390625, 0.00838470458984375, 0.0292205810546875, 0.02850341796875, -0.00406646728515625, -0.017486572265625, -0.006011962890625, 0.00959014892578125, 0.00408172607421875, 0.016632080078125, -0.0101470947265625, -0.0230255126953125, -0.0026874542236328125, -0.016754150390625, -0.01194000244140625, 0.002254486083984375, -0.01459503173828125, 0.0198822021484375, -0.04071044921875, 0.0010061264038085938, -0.0182952880859375, -0.03692626953125, 0.0069122314453125, -0.033355712890625, 0.0012798309326171875, -0.0186309814453125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving experiences in a structured memory bank\u2014a repository of records summarizing past tasks, actions, and outcomes. In this work, we define memory-based self-improving agents as those that (1) explicitly maintain a persistent, structured memory of prior experiences in textual or vectorized form, (2) use this memory to inform future decision-making, and (3) update their memory online as new tasks are encountered. This definition encompasses a broad class of agents, including retrieval-augmented language model agents, memory-augmented reinforcement learning (RL) agents, and other architectures that integrate explicit memory mechanisms. \n\nWe focus on two representative and widely studied methods: (1) a retrieval-augmented language model agent, which combines large language models with explicit memory retrieval (cf. [3]), and (2) a memory-augmented RL agent, which extends deep RL with episodic memory modules (cf. [4]). These methods were selected due to their prevalence in recent literature, their explicit use of structured memory, and their relevance to domains such as automated customer support, adaptive tutoring, and autonomous robotics, where unpredictable failures can have significant real-world consequences (e.g., [1,2]). While other agent types exist (e.g., agents with implicit memory, or those using alternative memory architectures such as differentiable neural computers), our study aims to provide a focused analysis of fragility\u2014here defined as the sensitivity of agent performance to stochasticity (randomness in environment or agent behavior) and task ordering (the sequence in which tasks are presented). This notion of fragility is motivated by prior work on robustness and generalization in sequential decision-making systems (see, e.g., [5,6]), where performance can degrade sharply under small changes in environment or training conditions. We acknowledge that a comprehensive taxonomy of memory-based agents and a systematic review of fragility definitions are beyond the scope of this study, but our focus is intended to enable tractable, reproducible experiments and clear analysis.\n\nMethods\n\nWe evaluate two agent architectures:\n\n- **Method A:** A retrieval-augmented language model agent using a transformer-based model (12-layer, 768 hidden units, 12 attention heads, similar to [3]) that selects actions based on current observations and retrieved textual memories. The memory bank stores textual summaries of prior episodes, each entry containing the task description, key actions taken, and observed outcomes. Retrieval is performed using dense vector similarity (cosine similarity over mean-pooled transformer embeddings), returning the top-5 most similar entries for each decision step. The memory module is implemented as a fixed-size list (max 500 entries); when full, the oldest entries are pruned (FIFO policy). If multiple entries are highly similar (cosine similarity > 0.98), only the most recent is retained to reduce redundancy.\n\n- **Method B:** A memory-augmented RL agent extending a deep Q-network (DQN) with an external episodic memory module (cf. [4]). The DQN uses a 3-layer MLP (256 units per layer, ReLU activations) to process observations. The memory module stores tuples of (state, action, reward, next state, textual summary), and retrieval is performed via nearest-neighbor search in embedding space (Euclidean distance over learned state representations), returning the top-3 matches per step. The memory bank is capped at 500 entries, with oldest entries pruned. If conflicting entries (identical state, different outcomes) are found, both are retained but flagged for manual review.\n\n**Memory Entry Generation and Validation:** Textual summaries are generated using a templated extraction process: for each episode, the agent logs the initial task description, a stepwise list of actions, and the final outcome. Key variables and constraints are extracted using regular expressions and environment-provided metadata. Summaries are validated by checking for the presence of all required fields (task, actions, outcome); incomplete entries are discarded. In the rubric-augmented condition, the extraction template is expanded to require explicit mention of all task-relevant variables and constraints, as determined by environment feedback.\n\n**Handling Redundancy and Conflicts:** When new entries are added, the memory module checks for near-duplicates (cosine similarity > 0.98 for Method A, Euclidean distance < 0.05 for Method B) and merges or discards as appropriate. If conflicting outcomes are observed for the same state-action pair, both entries are retained with a conflict flag.\n\n**Simulated Environments:** Agents operate in two types of simulated environments:\n\n1. **Sequential puzzle solving:** Tower of Hanoi (3\u20135 disks), logic grid puzzles (3\u20135 variables). Observations are structured as textual descriptions of the current state; actions are discrete (e.g., \"move disk X to peg Y\" or \"assign value Z to variable Y\"). Task complexity is operationalized as the minimum number of steps required for optimal solution (3\u201315 steps for Hanoi, 5\u201312 for logic puzzles).\n2. **Navigation challenges:** 10x10 gridworld mazes with dynamic obstacles, stochastic movement, and randomized goal locations. Observations are 2D arrays (agent, obstacles, goal locations); actions are discrete (up, down, left, right). Task complexity is defined by the shortest path length (8\u201320 steps) and the number of dynamic obstacles (1\u20135).\n\nEach environment is parameterized by task complexity, stochasticity (probability of random action/environmental change per step, set to 0.1 unless otherwise noted), and reward structure (positive reward for task completion, negative reward for failed or invalid actions). For each environment type, we generated a benchmark of 20 tasks using fixed random seeds for reproducibility. Task orderings were randomized across runs to assess curriculum effects. Benchmark tasks were generated by sampling environment parameters uniformly within the specified ranges and verifying solvability via automated checks.\n\n**Agent Hyperparameters:** Hyperparameters were set following [3,4]: learning rates of 1e-4 (Method A) and 5e-4 (Method B), batch size 32, memory bank size 500. No additional hyperparameter tuning or sensitivity analysis was performed; these values were chosen for comparability with prior work and to ensure agents could complete at least 10% of tasks in preliminary runs. We acknowledge that this minimal validation criterion is arbitrary and that lack of tuning may affect performance variability.\n\n**Evaluation Metrics:** Performance is measured by (1) task completion rate (proportion of tasks completed within 50 steps for puzzles, 100 steps for navigation), and (2) average reward (mean cumulative reward per episode). Metrics are reported as means and standard deviations across five independent random seeds per agent-task pair.\n\n**Statistical Analysis:** We use paired t-tests to compare agent performance across conditions (e.g., curriculum vs. random task order), reporting p-values, 95% confidence intervals, and Cohen\u2019s d effect sizes for all comparisons. Given the small sample size (n=5), statistical power is limited and effect size estimates are imprecise; results should be interpreted with caution. Normality of completion rates and rewards was assessed using the Shapiro-Wilk test (all p > 0.05); where non-normality was detected, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests were used for confirmation. Holm-Bonferroni correction was applied for multiple comparisons. We do not claim statistical significance in the strict sense and report effect sizes to contextualize findings.\n\n**Manual Analysis of Memory Entries:** To probe the causes of fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by randomly sampling 50 memory entries per agent (stratified across tasks and runs). Two annotators independently categorized each entry using a rubric with three axes: specificity (granularity of action/state description: high, medium, low), relevance (direct applicability to current task: direct, partial, irrelevant), and completeness (coverage of task requirements and constraints: complete, partial, missing). Disagreements were resolved through joint review and consensus. Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s \u03ba (0.82, considered \"almost perfect\" per [7]). The sample size is limited and may not capture all memory patterns; results should be interpreted as indicative rather than exhaustive.\n\nTo validate the impact of underspecification, we augmented the memory construction process by requiring entries to explicitly specify all relevant variables and constraints, guided by a detailed rubric and environment feedback. This intervention was implemented by modifying the templated extraction process to include all task-relevant details.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy: for complex, multi-step tasks, the standard deviation of completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for Method A and 15% for Method B. For example, in the navigation environment, Method A achieved a mean completion rate of 68% (SD = 11.7%, 95% CI [60.2%, 75.8%]), while Method B achieved 62% (SD = 14.9%, 95% CI [51.1%, 72.9%]). Introducing the self-improving loop amplifies this variance, with some runs failing to improve over time. Second, agent improvement is highly dependent on task order: when tasks are presented in a random order, average completion rates drop by 18% (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.21, p = 0.004, Holm-Bonferroni corrected), compared to the default curriculum order, indicating a strong curriculum dependency.\n\n**Manual Analysis of Memory Entries:** We observed frequent underspecification: 44% of entries lacked critical details about task requirements or environment constraints. We define underspecification as the omission of information necessary for accurate task execution or generalization. For example, an underspecified entry might read \"Avoid obstacles,\" omitting which obstacles or under what conditions. Inter-rater agreement on the rubric axes was high (\u03ba ="}}